3. CREATING YOUR OWN GITHUB REPOSITORY
============================================================

3.1 Introduction

Now let's upload an existing local project to GitHub.

Example project:

    python-for-ai

The objective is to:
- Create a GitHub repository.
- Connect the local project to GitHub.
- Upload the project files.
- Maintain the project using Git.

IMPORTANT:
Before pushing your project to GitHub, create a ".gitignore" file.

This prevents unnecessary files, sensitive information, and local environment folders from being uploaded.

============================================================
4. UNDERSTANDING THE .gitignore FILE
============================================================

4.1 What is .gitignore?

A ".gitignore" file tells Git which files and directories should not be tracked.

It helps prevent unnecessary files from being included in your Git commits.

For Python projects, common files and folders to ignore include:

- .venv/
- .env
- __pycache__/
- *.pyc
- .DS_Store

4.2 Why is .gitignore Important?

Consider a Python project containing a virtual environment.

The ".venv" directory may contain many installed packages and can become very large.

Uploading it to GitHub:
- Increases repository size.
- Wastes storage space.
- May cause push failures.
- Makes project sharing inefficient.

Similarly, ".env" files may contain sensitive information such as API keys and passwords.

Therefore, these files should not be committed to GitHub.

4.3 Create a .gitignore File

Step 1: Open your project folder.

Step 2: Create a new file named:

    .gitignore

Step 3: Add the following content:

    # Python virtual environment
    .venv/

    # Environment variables and secrets
    .env

    # Python cache files
    __pycache__/
    *.pyc

    # macOS system files
    .DS_Store

Step 4: Save the file in the root directory of your project.

Example project structure:

    python-for-ai/
        .gitignore
        main.py
        requirements.txt
        .venv/
        __pycache__/

Git will ignore the matching files and directories that are not already tracked.

IMPORTANT:
Adding a file to ".gitignore" does not automatically remove it if Git is already tracking it.

Also, never upload API keys, passwords, or other secrets to GitHub.

============================================================
5. CREATE A REPOSITORY ON GITHUB
============================================================

Step 1: Open GitHub.

Visit:

    https://github.com/

Step 2: Click the green "New" button.

Alternatively:

1. Click the "+" icon in the top-right corner.
2. Select "New repository".

Step 3: Configure the Repository.

Enter the following details:

Repository Name:

    python-for-ai

Description:

    Learning Python for AI development

Visibility:

    Private

Choose Private for now if you want to restrict access to your repository.

Public:
Anyone can view the repository.

Private:
Only you and authorized collaborators can access the repository.

Step 4: Initialize Repository Options.

For this workflow, do not select:

- Add a README file.
- Add .gitignore.
- Choose a license.

Reason:

Your project already exists locally, and you will upload its contents.

Creating these files on GitHub first can introduce additional setup steps when connecting the local and remote repositories.

Step 5: Click "Create repository".

Your GitHub repository is now created.

============================================================
6. CONNECT YOUR LOCAL PROJECT TO GITHUB
============================================================

6.1 Introduction

After creating the GitHub repository, the next step is to connect your local project to the remote repository.

This allows you to upload your local code to GitHub.

6.2 Choose an Authentication Method

You can connect using:

1. HTTPS
2. SSH

The following example uses HTTPS.

6.3 Initialize Git in Your Local Project

Step 1: Open the Terminal.

Step 2: Navigate to your "python-for-ai" project directory.

macOS:

    cd ~/Documents/python-for-ai

Windows:

    cd C:\Users\YourName\Documents\python-for-ai

Step 3: Initialize Git.

    git init

This initializes a new Git repository in your project directory.

Step 4: Add Project Files.

    git add .

This stages all eligible files in the current directory, excluding files ignored by ".gitignore".

Step 5: Create Your First Commit.

    git commit -m "Initial commit"

This creates the first commit in your local repository.

Step 6: Rename the Branch to main.

    git branch -M main

This renames the current branch to "main".

Step 7: Connect the Local Repository to GitHub.

    git remote add origin https://github.com/YOUR-USERNAME/python-for-ai.git

Replace "YOUR-USERNAME" with your actual GitHub username.

This command connects your local repository to the GitHub repository using the remote name "origin".

Step 8: Push Your Code to GitHub.

    git push -u origin main

This uploads your local commits to GitHub.

The "-u" option sets the upstream tracking relationship between your local "main" branch and the remote "main" branch.

After this setup, you can generally use "git push" without specifying the branch.

6.4 Complete Command Sequence

Execute the following commands from your project directory:

    git init

    git add .

    git commit -m "Initial commit"

    git branch -M main

    git remote add origin https://github.com/YOUR-USERNAME/python-for-ai.git

    git push -u origin main

IMPORTANT:
Make sure that:
- You are inside the correct project directory.
- Your ".gitignore" file is created.
- Your Git username and email are configured.
- Your GitHub repository has been created.
- Your authentication is configured correctly.

6.5 GitHub Authentication During Push

When pushing through HTTPS, GitHub may request authentication.

IMPORTANT:
GitHub does not accept your normal account password for Git operations over HTTPS.

You must use an appropriate authentication method, such as:

- GitHub CLI authentication.
- Personal Access Token (PAT).
- Git Credential Manager or another supported credential manager.

If GitHub CLI is already configured, it can simplify authentication.

============================================================
7. DAILY GIT WORKFLOW
============================================================

7.1 Introduction

Once your project is uploaded to GitHub, you do not need to repeat the complete initialization and repository connection process.

For everyday development, you mainly use three commands.

7.2 Daily Workflow

Step 1: Make Changes to Your Code.

Modify your Python files or add new features.

Step 2: Stage Your Changes.

    git add .

This stages the changes for the next commit.

Step 3: Commit Your Changes.

    git commit -m "Add new feature"

This saves the changes locally.

Step 4: Push Changes to GitHub.

    git push

This uploads your committed changes to GitHub.

7.3 Complete Daily Command Sequence

    git add .

    git commit -m "Add new feature"

    git push

This is the basic workflow you will use frequently when working with Git.

IMPORTANT:
A commit saves changes locally. A push uploads those commits to the remote GitHub repository.


